WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is a "New York
Times" best-selling author

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:04.204
and research professor

00:00:04.204 --> 00:00:07.573
who has a brand-new
weekly podcast on Spotify

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:09.141
called "Dare to Lead."

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:11.544
Here is Brene Brown.

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.546
Yeah, Brene.
It's so nice to see you.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:14.880
Thank you so much
for coming back...

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.016
-Thank you.
-...to the show.

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:20.786
I appreciate this. I want
to talk about this new podcast.

00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:23.722
It's about leadership,
called "Dare to Lead."

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:27.193
I don't know. I just feel like
it's a good message for anyone,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.596
because one of your messages
in your book that I read earlier

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:35.935
is that every one of us
is a leader in some way.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.770
It's not just
for people in charge.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.605
Like, everyone --

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.373
Can you expand on that?

00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:43.142
-Yeah. I mean, I think
when we think "leader,"

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:45.712
we think, like, pinstripes
and shoulder pads

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.646
and a "C" suite office,

00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:49.615
and the truth is,
I've been in those offices

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.318
and couldn't find a leader
to save my life.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.353
And I've been around
everyday people

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:56.289
that would never identify
as a leader

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:57.657
and been surrounded by them.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:00:59.558
So I define a leader

00:00:59.558 --> 00:01:03.062
as any person who holds
themselves accountable

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.564
for finding the potential
in people and processes

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:08.868
and has the courage, the guts
to develop that potential.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.504
And that's all of us.
I mean, we're just --

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.840
I recently asked Jon Meacham,
I said, "What would you say

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.541
to people who don't think
they're leaders?"

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:18.411
And he said, "Then you don't
understand democracy.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:19.812
We all have to lead."
-Wow.

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.314
-And so I just -- I loved that.

00:01:22.314 --> 00:01:23.817
-Yeah.
-I think we're all leaders.

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:27.020
-You've interviewed all these
people and tried to find --

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:31.090
and you've studied leaders,
and you try to find patterns.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.993
What are a few patterns
that you always see showing up

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.661
in good leaders?

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:37.096
-Yeah.
This was the shock for me.

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:40.934
In fact, this is my 23rd year
as a researcher,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.334
and this is a 10-year study.

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:44.937
And it was the first study
I'd ever done

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:48.107
where the answers across
all of the research participants

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:51.143
was the same. So, what we asked
was really straightforward.

00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:53.479
From special forces military
to creatives

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:56.682
to all over the world, NGOs,
we asked,

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.150
"What's the future
of leadership?

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:00.687
Who's going to be leading
in 5 years, 10 years,

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.155
and who's going to be gone?"

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:05.858
And the answer was,
"We need braver leaders."

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:07.627
And so when we drilled into,
what does that mean?

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.696
I mean, I love the word "brave."
I love the word "courage,"

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:10.696
but they're gauzy.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:12.098
Like, what does that mean?
-Yeah.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.534
-It came down
to four skill sets --

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:17.469
the ability to rumble
with vulnerability.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:20.506
You know, that means when things
get uncertain and hard,

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:22.141
we don't tap out.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.376
We don't tap out
of hard conversations.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:27.313
We don't tap out of feedback,
giving it or receiving it.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.015
-Mm-hmm.
-Second thing is

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:32.785
that we really learn
how to live into our values.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:36.088
I mean, the most daring leaders
we've talked to

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:39.359
are very clear not only
about what their values are,

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:42.962
but also what the behaviors are
that support those values.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.698
Like, there are a lot
of big, fun leadership words

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:46.866
like "integrity."

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.934
But, like, if I walk
into another organization

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.404
and see an integrity, like,
poster with an eagle,

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:52.772
like, what does that --

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:55.241
You know, it's like, "Caw!"

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.076
What does that mean?
-I don't know what it means.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.978
You were on Skymall,
and you were bored,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:02.081
and you go it's only 10 bucks.
-[ Laughs ] Skymall.

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:03.416
No, Skymall.

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.051
And you don't know
what it means.

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:07.453
So these leaders
have operationalized it

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:10.122
into behaviors that they
hold themselves accountable for.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.257
The third thing is
building trust,

00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:13.993
talking explicitly about trust,

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.962
'cause no trust,
no teams, no good work.

00:03:16.962 --> 00:03:19.499
And the last one is huge.
And I learned this

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:22.501
from working with a lot
of professional sports teams.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.637
You've got to know how to reset

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.641
after failure, disappointment,
and setbacks.

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.342
You got to learn how to
get back up when you fall,

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:31.911
'cause that's part
of being brave.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:33.779
-Today is Veterans Day,

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:37.150
and I know you've worked
with a lot of military leaders.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.919
You do a lot of great stuff
for the military.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:43.956
What have you learned
from military specifically?

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:45.357
-You know, it's really --

00:03:45.357 --> 00:03:48.494
You know, when you're a person
who teaches vulnerability,

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:50.863
going in to work with
special forces

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.131
or, you know,
Air Force fighter pilots,

00:03:53.131 --> 00:03:56.469
you're like, "Oh, man, this is
going to be a hard sell."

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:59.772
But what's interesting
is they probably taught me more

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:04.043
about the importance
of being in uncertainty.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:07.780
I think it's like, when you got
to be all blustery at work,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.549
it's harder --
-It's not going to work.

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:11.650
-Yeah. When your life's
on the line,

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.454
you get the importance
of emotion vulnerability.

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:17.657
-You know, you end every episode
on your podcast

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:18.925
asking your guests

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.093
to name five songs
that they can't live without,

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:22.594
which I think is tough.

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:26.132
And you ask what that playlist
says about them, and I love it.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.134
I think it's a great,
great question.

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:30.503
So I thought maybe I'd turn
the tables if you're up for it

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:31.804
and ask you.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:32.905
-Okay. Well,
I thought about this,

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.540
and these are not songs
that I just love.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.108
These are songs
that I would be sad

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:38.144
if I couldn't find them
when I needed them.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.278
-Wow.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.381
-Yeah. And so
they're real for me.

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:44.650
So, "If I Needed You,"
Townes Van Zandt.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.786
"Amazing Grace," Willie Nelson.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:49.555
"Before the Last Teardrop
Falls," Freddy Fender.

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:51.123
-What? [ Laughs ]

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:52.958
-Yes, for sure.

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:55.060
-I love Freddy Fender.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:00.833
Bonnie Raitt and John Prine
singing "Angel from Montgomery."

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:05.872
And Carol Woods and Timothy
Mitchum's cover of "Let It Be."

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:07.573
I have to have those songs.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.207
-Wow.
-When the going get's tough,

00:05:09.207 --> 00:05:10.476
those songs get going.

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:13.012
-I really appreciate
you being back on the show.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.213
Thank you so much.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.515
-Thank you.
-Please be well.

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:18.551
Brene Brown. New episodes
of "Dare to Lead" --

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:21.220
"Dare to Lead" are released
every Monday on Spotify.

